# How to publish a new blog post

No build step, no Node, no local setup required. Just:

1. Copy \`_posts/2026-07-26-welcome-to-the-oasis-blog.md\` as a template.
2. Create a new file in \`_posts/\` named \`YYYY-MM-DD-your-title-slug.md\` (the date in the filename controls the publish date and sort order — it must come first).
3. Fill in the front matter and write the body in Markdown:

   \`\`\`markdown
   ---
   title: "Your post title"
   tags: [release]
   ---

   Your first paragraph becomes the excerpt shown on /blog/ and in the
   homepage teaser, so make it count.

   ## A subheading

   Regular Markdown from here: **bold**, _italic_, lists, images, code
   blocks, links — all supported.
   \`\`\`
4. Run the project checks, then commit and push to the default branch.

GitHub Pages rebuilds the site automatically. The post appears on \`/blog/\`, in the RSS feed and sitemap, and in the homepage blog teaser once it is among the three newest posts.

### Optional front matter

- \`title\`: required post title.
- \`tags\`: one primary tag, such as \`release\`, \`announcement\`, \`deep-dive\` or \`guide\`.

### Preview locally

Requires Ruby and Bundler:

\`\`\`powershell
bundle install
bundle exec jekyll serve
\`\`\`

Open \`http://localhost:4000/O.A.S.I.S./blog/\`.

### Validate before publishing

1. Confirm the filename starts with an ISO date: \`YYYY-MM-DD-\`.
2. Check the front matter parses correctly.
3. Check internal links and images.
4. Review the rendered post at \`/blog/\`.
5. Confirm the RSS feed and sitemap include the post.

### Useful paths

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| \`_posts/\` | Blog source posts |
| \`_layouts/post.html\` | Post template |
| \`_layouts/default.html\` | Shared blog layout |
| \`blog/\` | Feed, index and generated output |
| \`Gemfile\` | Jekyll dependencies |
| \`sync-web.ps1\` | Full repository publish flow |

For product issues, use [GitHub Issues](https://github.com/OASISLocal/O.A.S.I.S./issues). For website publishing changes, keep commits focused and verify the live page after deployment.
